      "title": "Govt mulls ATF price stabilisation fund for airlines amid rising fuel costs",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/industry/aviation/govt-mulls-atf-price-stabilisation-fund-for-airlines-amid-rising-fuel-costs-126100600835_1.html",
      "description": "Amid rising jet fuel prices, the government is considering whether to revive the price stabilisation fund and is also in discussions with airlines as well as oil marketing companies on the high ATF costs.\nAviation Turbine Fuel (ATF) accounts for around 40 per cent of an airline's operational expenses, and with the continuing West Asia turmoil, fuel prices have been on the rise.\n\"One of the most important things is how the West Asia crisis is putting a big burden on ATF prices. We are in discussions with airlines and OMCs (Oil Marketing Companies),\"  Naidu said on the sidelines of a conference in the national capital on Tuesday.\nThe government is considering whether to have the price stabilisation fund for the ATF, according to a senior official.\nSpecific details could not be immediately ascertained.\nIn June, the Union Cabinet cleared a one-time budgetary support not exceeding Rs 10,000 crore for Oil Marketing Companies (OMCs) to provide ATF price stabilisation in order to provide",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-10-06T17:18:27"
    },
    {
      "title": "Airlines warn govt of route cuts as soaring ATF costs squeeze finances",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/industry/aviation/airlines-warn-govt-of-route-cuts-as-soaring-atf-costs-squeeze-finances-126100601039_1.html",
      "description": "Airlines seek changes in ATF pricing and taxes, lower state VAT and extended relief on airport charges as the West Asia conflict drives up operating costs",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
