    {
      "title": "Federation of Indian Airlines seeks review of existing ATF pricing formula",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/industry/aviation/federation-of-indian-airlines-seeks-review-of-existing-atf-pricing-formula-126100600635_1.html",
      "description": "The Federation of Indian Airlines (FIA) is seeking a review of the existing jet fuel pricing formula, and said domestic ATF should be priced on the actual cost of fuel plus a reasonable margin rather than international benchmark pricing.\nThe unprecedented increase in fuel costs, compounded by geopolitical disruptions and rupee depreciation, is placing significant pressure on airline cash flows and viability, it said.\nIn a letter to the Civil Aviation Ministry late last month, FIA also said that \"if timely relief is not available, the airlines may be compelled to withdraw from several unsustainable routes.\"\nAir India, IndiGo and SpiceJet are the members of FIA.\nFrom around 30-40 per cent, airlines' operating costs have increased to around 55-60 per cent under the prevailing circumstances, out of which ATF remains the single largest variable cost, it said.\nThe current conflict (West Asia) has pushed the Brent Crude from USD 72 / BBL to USD 118 / BBL, and the resultant ATF price (MOP",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-10-06T09:53:36"
    },
    {
      "title": "Govt in talks with airlines, OMCs over high ATF prices: Aviation minister",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/industry/aviation/govt-in-talks-with-airlines-omcs-over-high-atf-prices-aviation-minister-126100600292_1.html",
      "description": "Civil Aviation Minister K Rammohan Naidu on Tuesday said the ministry is in discussions with airlines and oil marketing companies on high jet fuel prices due to the West Asia crisis.\nAirlines are facing headwinds due to the rising prices of Aviation Turbine Fuel (ATF), which accounts for around 40 per cent of a carrier's operational costs.\n\"One of the most important things is how the West Asia crisis is putting a big burden on ATF prices. We are in discussions with airlines and OMCs (Oil Marketing Companies),\"  Naidu said on the sidelines of a conference in the national capital on Tuesday.\nOn Monday, the country's largest airline IndiGo announced a hike in fuel surcharge for domestic and international flights in the wake of rising jet fuel prices.\nIndiGo had said ATF prices have remained volatile in recent months, especially following the geopolitical situation in the Middle East.\nAccording to the airline, the continuous rise in fuel prices, with the latest month-on-month increase",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-10-06T06:01:47"
    }
